# Environment Variables

## Required for Production

### Database
- `DATABASE_URL` - PostgreSQL connection string (provided by Railway Postgres plugin)
  - Format: `postgresql://user:password@host:port/dbname`
  - Railway automatically injects this when you link the Postgres plugin

### Authentication
- `NEXTAUTH_URL` - Public URL of your application
  - Example: `https://your-app.up.railway.app`
  - Used for OAuth redirects and session callbacks
  - Must match your actual public URL

- `NEXTAUTH_SECRET` - Cryptographically secure random string for session encryption
  - Generate: `openssl rand -base64 32`
  - Do NOT commit this to version control
  - Should be unique per environment

### Optional (S3-compatible storage)
- `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` - AWS or S3-compatible storage access key
- `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` - Corresponding secret key
- `AWS_REGION` - S3 region (e.g., `us-east-1`)
- `S3_BUCKET` - Bucket name for storing photos

## Development (.env.local)

```bash
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/photo_delivery"
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"
NEXTAUTH_SECRET="your-test-secret-key-here"
```

## Deployment (Railway)

1. Create a PostgreSQL plugin in Railway
2. Set environment variables in Railway dashboard:
   - `NEXTAUTH_URL` = your app's public URL
   - `NEXTAUTH_SECRET` = generated random string
3. Set optional S3 variables if using external storage
4. DATABASE_URL is automatically provided by the Postgres plugin

## Per-Environment

| Variable | Dev | Production | Required |
|----------|-----|------------|----------|
| DATABASE_URL | ✅ postgres://localhost | ✅ Railway Postgres | ✅ Yes |
| NEXTAUTH_URL | http://localhost:3000 | https://your-app.railway.app | ✅ Yes |
| NEXTAUTH_SECRET | test-secret | `openssl rand -base64 32` | ✅ Yes |
| AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID | - | (if using S3) | ❌ No |
| AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY | - | (if using S3) | ❌ No |
